                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1338-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Ehrlund Microphones EHR-B</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The EHR-B is the &#8220;broadcast&#8221; version of G&ouml;ran Ehrlund&#8217;s EHR-M condenser mic. Both are premium, fixed-Cardioid, transformerless condenser microphones with the company&#8217;s unique (and patented) triangular capsule design. </p><p>The only difference between the two mics is the inclusion of a fixed high-pass filter (at 80Hz) on the B version, which is built specifically for broadcast use.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/ehrlund/EHR-M-capsule.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/ehrlund/EHR-M-capsule-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="EHR-M Capsule" width="133" /></a>The mic&#8217;s triangular capsule is said to be less subject to resonance than a circular diaphragm would be. Read more in the patent, linked from the sidebar, or in the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Ehrlund/EHR-M">EHR-M profile</a>.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Ehrlund/EHR-B">Ehrlund Microphones EHR-B</a></p>
